WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.500
BETH: Depending on the level of ability
and skills an individual with autism has,

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:09.250
they can once they gain employment, become
a valued member of a company’s workforce.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.320
They can offer loyalty, dedication and
can be extremely skillful once trained.

00:00:14.839 --> 00:00:18.110
They may even have advanced skills to
offer if they are high functioning.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.619
JOE: However, they may find the usual
process of gaining and starting employment

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:26.999
very challenging because of their disorder
and not their ability to do the job.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.860
If they are supported through
the process by both the employer

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.330
and their carers, they can find
seeking and gaining employment

00:00:34.389 --> 00:00:36.459
easier to navigate and understand.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.160
BETH: The more employers are open to
taking on individuals with autism,

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.330
the more they will understand
their needs, and the skills and

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:44.970
expertise they have to offer.

00:00:45.519 --> 00:00:49.389
It is important that employers realise
that many individuals with autism do

00:00:49.389 --> 00:00:54.129
not see themselves as ‘disabled’ but
‘neurodiverse’ meaning they just look at

00:00:54.129 --> 00:00:58.510
life differently, so they can also bring
this original thinking to a job role.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.269
JOE: High functioning individuals can
be very successful in many different

00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:08.580
fields including science, research,
finance, banking and the arts.

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.910
If employers are more open to looking
at candidates who are on the spectrum,

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.450
they have opportunities to make their
workforce more diverse, with a chance

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:20.230
that they may become their best employees.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.890
BETH: Individuals with autism can
be supported by employers offering

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.140
paid work placements to see how
individuals fit into their workforce.

00:01:28.699 --> 00:01:32.479
This can help them to make any adjustments
that might be needed before offering

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.420
a full-time role, and it can give
individuals with autism a step into

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.220
employment, to see what it involves
and gain that valuable work experience.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.119
JOE: Making the application process for
individuals with autism, simpler and more

00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:48.260
inclusive will help in the beginning.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.500
This can be done by companies
becoming more autism aware,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.870
especially about the skills
individuals with autism have to offer.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.989
Being clear in the job application
and interview process, can support

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:03.509
individuals directly, making it
easier for them to feel that they

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.630
have a chance of gaining employment.

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.099
BETH: Being aware of sensory
sensitivities and communication

00:02:09.109 --> 00:02:13.010
difficulties, can help prepare them
for the differences individuals present

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.129
in social interaction, such as in the
interview and job induction phase.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:21.589
Also going forward to when they accept
a role within a company, their sensory

00:02:21.589 --> 00:02:23.630
needs within their working environment.

00:02:24.279 --> 00:02:28.260
Little changes and reasonable adjustments
can make a lot of difference to an

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:32.010
individual with autism and can help
them to settle in a job and become

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.109
a valued member of the workforce.

